        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - '11.4 - 2.32' (start)] A subtraction problem is written in red on the whiteboard: 11.4 is written above 2.32, with a red line underneath. Below the line, a partially written answer '5' and other illegible numbers are visible.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 100 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The whiteboard is cleared.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 32 seconds] The whiteboard appears, showing a drawing of Mickey Mouse. The tutor then immediately clears the board.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 24 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE: Opens whiteboard] The screen changes to a blank whiteboard with a drawing of a red panda-like creature in red and black ink.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: Hi.\nTurn 2. STUDENT: Hi.\nTurn 3. TUTOR: How are you?\nTurn 4. STUDENT: Good.\nTurn 5. TUTOR: How was your weekend?\nTurn 6. STUDENT: Good.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: Have you done anything fun?\nTurn 8. STUDENT: Um, no.\nTurn 9. TUTOR: How was your brother's birthday?\nTurn 10. STUDENT: Oh yeah. Um, we went, um, to this place where it's like a bowling place and, um, but my brother didn't want to bowl, so we just went to the arcade.\nTurn 11. TUTOR: Mhm.\nTurn 12. STUDENT: There. And we got a lot of prizes. And then we went to this sushi place that, uh, sometimes the robot would like bring you your drinks.\nTurn 13. TUTOR: Oh.\nTurn 14. STUDENT: And you would order on a tablet.\nTurn 15. TUTOR: Mhm.\nTurn 16. STUDENT: And then, um, we went home.\nTurn 17. TUTOR: That's cool.\nTurn 18. TUTOR: Do you like sushi?\nTurn 19. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 20. TUTOR: Oh, and then...\nTurn 21. TUTOR: What kind of sushi do you like?\nTurn 22. STUDENT: California roll.\nTurn 23. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 24. TUTOR: Nice.\nTurn 25. STUDENT: And then, uh, after we went to get sushi, we went to this place, uh, like, I think it's like a park. Mhm. There, the water was like pink, but like it dried out, so you could only see the salt, like the sea salt.\nTurn 26. TUTOR: Wow. That's cool.\nTurn 27. TUTOR: Yeah, I haven't done anything interesting this week. I've just been working and, yeah.\nTurn 28. TUTOR: Okay, so we'll get started with our check-in activity. Is there anything specific you want to do?\nTurn 29. STUDENT: Uh, the drawing game.\nTurn 30. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 31. TUTOR: Do you want me to draw first?\nTurn 32. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 34. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 29 seconds]\nTurn 34. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - drawing] Tutor draws a picture of a fairy on the whiteboard. The fairy has a green dress, blonde