dent - drawing] Student draws a tail with stripes.\nTurn 41. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 7 seconds]\nTurn 41. STUDENT: I'm done.\nTurn 42. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 3 seconds]\nTurn 42. TUTOR: Okay. I think it's the red panda from Turning Red.\nTurn 43. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 44. TUTOR: That was really good.\nTurn 45. TUTOR: Okay. So now we'll get started in the learning platform. Is there anything specific you want to work on today or should we just pick a random lesson?\nTurn 46. STUDENT: A random lesson.\nTurn 47. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 48. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The whiteboard is replaced with a learning platform interface.\nTurn 48. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - scrolling] The tutor scrolls through a list of math topics, including \"Independent learning,\" \"Question of the Day,\" \"Introduction to coordinates,\" \"Name and plot coordinates 1,\" \"Graphing and interpreting points in the first quadrant,\" and others.\nTutor clicks on the \"Preview\" button for \"Geometrical reasoning with coordinates 1\". A pop-up window appears showing a sample problem.\nTurn 48. STUDENT: Geometrical reasoning with coordinates.\nTurn 49. TUTOR: Okay, let's look at the preview for that.\nTurn 50. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'Geometrical reasoning with coordinates 1' (start)] A pop-up shows a coordinate plane with a rectangle. The question is \"What is the coordinate of the missing vertex of the rectangle?\". The vertices are labeled (3, 12), (10, 12), (?, ?), and (10, 7). Multiple choice options are A: (7, 10), B: (7, 3), C: (3, 10), D: (3, 7).\nTutor closes the preview pop-up and clicks \"Start topic\".\nTurn 50. TUTOR: Okay. Yeah, this one looks good.\nTurn 51. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 52. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen loads the \"Geometrical reasoning with coordinates 1\" lesson.\nTurn 52. TUTOR: Okay, so this one says, what is the coordinate of the missing vertex of the rectangle? So we need to find what these two question marks are.\nTurn 53. TUTOR: So do you remember what X and Y are in the parentheses?\nTurn 54. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds] Tutor draws a vertical line labeled 'Y' and a horizontal line labeled 'X' on the problem's graph.\nSCREEN INTERACTION: tutor - drawing\nTutor draws a curve on the left side of the whiteboard.\nSCREEN INTERACTION: tutor - writing\nTutor writes \"(X,\" next to the curve.\nSCREEN INTERACTION: tutor - writing\nTutor completes the coordinate pair, writing \"(X, Y)\".\nSCREEN INTERACTION: tutor - highlighting\nTutor highlights the two left points of the rectangle, (3, 12) and (3, 7).\nSCREEN INTERACTION: tutor - writing\nTutor writes \"(3, \" on the left side of the whiteboard.\nSCREEN INTERACTION: tutor - highlighting\nTutor highlights the top two points of the rectangle, (3, 12) and (10, 12).\nTurn 54. STUDENT: The the Y axis is the one going up. The X axis is the one like this.\nTurn 55. TUTOR: Mhm.\nTurn 56. STUDENT: The x-axis is the one that one like this.\nTurn 57. TUTOR: Yep, good job.\nTurn 58. TUTOR: And then when we write it in parentheses, which comes first, X or Y?\nTurn 59. STUDENT: X.\nTurn 60. TUTOR: Good job.\nTurn 61. TUTOR: So, let's find X first.\nTurn 62. TUTOR: So we have these different points, and so we can find both of the question marks using the different points.\nTurn 63. TUTOR: So, let's try to find X first.\nTurn 64. TUTOR: So, where on the x-axis does it look like this is?\nTurn 65. STUDENT: Like, like two maybe?\nTurn 66. TUTOR: Two?\nTurn 67. TUTOR: So here's an easy way to figure this one out.\nTurn 68. TUTOR: So, these two points are connected and it kind of looks like they're on a straight line.\nTurn 69. TUTOR: So we can look at what X is for this coordinate, which is three, and we know that's also going to be our X coordinate.\nTurn 70. TUTOR: So our first coordinate is going to be three.\nTurn 71. TUTOR: Now kind of doing the same thing with Y, can we figure out what Y is?\nTurn 72. STUDENT: Seven.\nTurn 73. TUTOR: Yeah, good job.\nTurn 74. TUTOR: So our answer is D.\nTurn 75. TUTOR: Good job.\nTurn 76. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - clicking] Student clicks on option D, then the \"Check\" button. The screen shows \"D is correct!\".\nTurn 76. TUTOR: Okay, next question.\nTurn 77. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p2 - 'What is the width of this rectangle as indicated by the arrow?' (start)] The problem shows a rectangle on a coordinate plane with vertices at (3, 12), (10, 12), (3, 7), and (10, 7). An arrow indicates the width along the top edge. The options are A: 12, B: 7, C: 3, D: 13.\nTurn 77. STUDENT: B.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nFor this next reply, decide privately which of the following teaching policies fits the current learner evidence, then apply it. Use the current mathematical task and recent student work in the context of the full conversation. Agreement with a tutor-supplied answer is not proof of understanding. An independently corrected earlier error does not by itself justify more help.\nSCAFFOLDING: Provide targeted scaffolding now. Identify the specific unresolved obstacle in the student's current mathematical work. Offer only the smallest useful hint, representation, or clarification that makes the next step accessible. Use the learner's actual numbers and ideas when available. Leave the key calculation or inference for the student and invite one manageable next step. Do not give a full solution, stack several leading questions, repeat an explanation the learner already understands, or invent unseen worksheet details.\nRIGOR: Push for appropriate rigor now. Build on what the learner has actually demonstrated, not an answer the tutor supplied. Invite the student to justify a step, connect representations, check a claim, generalize, or take the next step independently. Choose one opportunity that fits the current problem. Do not give the reasoning before asking for it, turn the challenge into a sequence of tiny leading hints, or introduce unrelated difficulty. Keep the mathematical work with the learner; rigor is not simply saying why after every answer.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
